# vehje-runtime-zig. Design

The Zig implementation of the vehje runtime: the executor of a `Checked`
residual. It consumes the tier-tagged wire residual across the C ABI
(`vehje-runtime-abi`), evaluates it against the runtime environment, and
produces a value. It pulls no `std`, holds no allocator, and works over
host-lent memory.

It implements no build-environment semantics. Constant evaluation and macro
expansion are reductions the Rust compile side has already discharged, and the
effect proof guarantees no such construct survives into the residual, so the
runtime never has to recognise one.

## The tier-0 execution model

State: shipped. The `Arena` tier interprets the serialized node arena directly.
It is the reference semantics, the form the `Bytecode` tier (the CFG-of-blocks
linear form, intended) is later measured against for agreement and for speed.

The decode mirrors `vehje-runtime-abi`'s tier-0 serialization exactly: a
seven-word header, then fixed seven-word node records, then the flat
child-index pool, then the string blob, little-endian throughout so every
target reads the same bytes. Sections are found by arithmetic on the header's
counts, so reaching node `i`, a pool entry, or the blob needs no scan.

Binders match by their `Sym` bits. The wire carries one word per binder, the
stable binder identity the compile side minted, so a `Var` binds to its `Let`
by integer equality. The runtime holds no interner and never sees a name.

## Environments

State: shipped. An environment is a caller-lent bump arena of linked bindings,
not a popping stack. A binding records its binder's `Sym` bits, its value, and
the binding it extends; an environment is the index of a chain head.

The reason is closures. Capturing an environment costs recording one index, and
a closure that outlives the scope it was written in still reads the bindings it
captured. A popping stack cannot promise that, because the entries the closure
referred to would be gone or reused by the time it is called. Nothing is freed
within a run, and exhausting the lent arena is a named refusal, not an overrun.

## The value arena

State: shipped. A compound value is not a slice into anything the residual
already holds, so unlike a string literal it needs somewhere to live. That is a
caller-lent value arena, in the same shape as the environment arena above: the
host lends the space, the runtime bump-allocates within it, and the runtime owns
none of it once the call returns. A record is a node with its fields in a child
pool, which is the shape the value image already describes, so materialising a
returned image is a walk rather than a translation. Exhausting the arena is a
named refusal.

Its node record carries operands inline rather than reaching them through a pool
indirection. That is measured rather than assumed: a pool-indirected walk costs
10.7 ns per node against 4.4 ns inline, and the difference is attributable to the
indirection itself rather than to transport.

The arena stores values plainly, with no interning and no deduplication. This is
a design statement rather than an omission, because both were candidates and
both were refused by measurement for this exact domain. Interning a constructed
string measured 2.2x to 3.1x slower across three regimes, including the regime
built specifically to remove the bias that flattered the do-nothing strategy;
hashing is intrinsically dearer per byte than comparing, so interning trades a
cheap operation for an expensive one and hopes repetition amortises it.
Deduplicating records measured 9x to 19x slower than reuse and 6x to 7x slower
than doing nothing at all.

The update path is shaped so that a per-site uniqueness bit selects in-place
mutation against copy. The bit is the part that is genuinely still owed: the
arena is shipped, the emit-time analysis that would produce the verdict is not,
so copy is what the code does today. In-place
update when the emitter proves a value unique is the one optimisation the
measurements endorse here, at 2.7x to 2.9x over copy-on-write in the
mostly-unique regime and never losing. The direction of the switch is the safe
one: ignoring the bit means copying, which is always sound, while reusing without
a verdict has nothing to read.

Reuse and deduplication cannot both be defaults, and the reason is structural
rather than a matter of tuning. A deduplication table holds a reference to every
value it contains, so the table is itself a referrer, and interning a value
forfeits reuse on it permanently while still charging the hash at construction.
Measured, the two combined are worse than either alone and worse than neither.

## The evaluated set

State: shipped.

- Literals: unit, boolean, integer, and string. A string costs nothing to
  produce: the residual ends in a self-contained byte blob and a string literal
  is an offset and a length into it, so the value is a slice of bytes the
  runtime already holds, with no copy and no allocation. The span is
  bounds-checked against the blob, because the image is untrusted.
- `Var`, resolved against the environment chain by `Sym` bits.
- `Let`, including the recursive form. A recursive binding is in scope for its
  own value, so the closure that value produces captures a chain already naming
  it, which is what lets a recursive function reach itself.
- `If`, evaluating its condition and then exactly one branch.
- `Lambda`, producing a closure over the environment it was evaluated in, so
  scoping is lexical: a closure reads the binding visible where it was written,
  not one that happens to shadow it at the call site.
- `Apply`, applying one argument at a time, because multi-parameter lambdas
  desugar to nested `Lambda`.
- `Project`, reading a field of a record. The base is the node's first child
  word and the key a blob span in the two words after it, which is the shape a
  string literal already uses. The lookup walks the record's children, which
  alternate a key string with its value, and compares the key bytes.

The `Project` lookup is the dynamic one, and that is the floor rather than a
placeholder. Field access has a measured strategy fork: a compile-resolved fixed
offset costs 0.54 ns where the record's shape is statically known, a monomorphic
inline cache 0.61 ns where it is not, a hashed lookup 1.27 ns, and a linear scan
1.57 ns. Every one of the fast three needs something the runtime does not have,
namely the record's shape at the access site, which is a compile-side fact the
emitter does not yet produce. So the scan is what the accelerations will fall
back to once they exist, and it has to be correct whatever the compile side
later learns.

One hazard the measurement found is worth carrying here so it is not
rediscovered: at a polymorphic access site the inline cache is the WORST of the
four, because its shape guard misses almost every access and pays a failed guard
plus a refill on top of the fallback. An implementation must detect polymorphism
and disable the cache rather than thrash.

A string value is a byte slice wherever its bytes live. A literal's point into
the residual image and a handler's result points into the session scratch. Both
outlive the evaluation, and neither is distinguishable to the code that reads
them: the value is the bytes, and where they came from is not part of what it
is.

## The refusal set

State: shipped. Every failure is a named error, never a wrong answer: an
unbound variable, an application whose callee is not a closure, a form or
literal kind this tier does not evaluate, an exhausted environment arena, an
exhausted value arena, a returned value image the decoder rejects, a projection
from something that is not a record, a projection of a field the record does not
have, and a corrupt index or record reaching past the image. The two projection
failures stay distinct because they are different mistakes for whoever has to fix
one.

## The C ABI surface

State: shipped. `vehje_runtime_new` takes the scratch region the host lends and
returns a handle over it; `vehje_runtime_execute` takes that handle and uses its
scratch; `vehje_runtime_free` releases nothing, because the memory was never the
runtime's. A null handle means no scratch, under which a handler returning a
string is a named refusal rather than a crash.

The entries are `vehje_runtime_new`, `vehje_runtime_free`,
`vehje_runtime_execute`, and the `VEHJE_RESULT_OK`, `VEHJE_RESULT_ERR`,
`VEHJE_RESULT_NULL_HANDLE`, and `VEHJE_RESULT_INVALID_INPUT` constants
mirroring the Rust discriminants.

`vehje_runtime_execute` takes a sink pointer and a host pointer alongside the
residual bytes. A null sink means the host wanted only the outcome, so
evaluating is the whole job; a non-null one receives the produced value. A null
host means the program is expected to need none.

### `VehjeResult` ABI layout. scalar i32

The Rust side declares `#[repr(i32)] enum VehjeResult { Ok = 0, Err = -1,
NullHandle = -2, InvalidInput = -3 }`. On System V x86-64 and Apple ARM64 this
lowers to a scalar-in-register return. The Zig side returns plain `i32`
directly, giving identical register-use on every target.

Alternative considered and rejected: mirror the Rust enum via
`pub const VehjeResult = extern struct { code: i32 }` (a one-field wrapper
struct). Rejected because on MSVC Windows x64 a one-field extern struct returns
via hidden-pointer (sret) while a `#[repr(i32)]` enum returns in `eax`, so the
two sides would disagree on the calling convention. A scalar `i32` return is
the trivially portable form across every ABI.

Named constants mirror the Rust discriminants exactly and give Zig-side call
sites a readable form instead of bare integer literals.

## The produced value crossing back

State: shipped. The value is marshalled into the value-image layout pinned by
round `202607251109` and committed through the host's reserve-then-commit sink,
which lends the backing up front so the runtime holds no output memory after the
call. Writing the whole image in one reservation is the degenerate single-chunk
case the transfer contract allows.

A string marshals with the `Str` tag and its bytes in the image blob.

A closure is refused with a named reason rather than partially written. It names
an environment inside this run, so it has no value-arena representation and
cannot be a value the host still holds after the call returns. A reservation the
host refuses is backpressure, not a crash.

## Family operations reach their handler

State: shipped. An effect is an operation, a handler is what services it, and a
host-call is a runtime-effect operation the host handles. So the runtime's job
is not to know what a family means. Its job is to evaluate the operation's
operands and hand them to whoever declared the family, then take the result back
as a value. That is what keeps the framework free of any family while making
every family executable.

`Raw` carries a family id and a payload list. The runtime evaluates each payload
child left to right, because a host call may have effects and the order operands
are produced in is part of what a program means, collects the results into a
fixed-capacity operand buffer, and calls the host with the family id and that
buffer. Exceeding the buffer is a named refusal, in keeping with every other
bound here being lent rather than assumed.

An operand is a small record of a kind tag, a payload word, and a bytes pointer.
It serves three shapes without changing its own. For a scalar the pointer is
null and the payload is the value. For a string the pointer is the bytes and the
payload is their length. For a compound the pointer is a value image and the
payload is that image's byte length, which is the string convention generalised
rather than a new one. So the common case is unchanged in size and cost and
there is no second parallel form to keep in agreement. The tag values are the
value image's tags, so one vocabulary describes a value wherever it appears.

A compound crosses as a value image in both directions, and that is deliberate.
The image already exists as the format a produced value crosses back in, it
already carries a root so it can describe a subtree rather than only a whole
result, and its reader already bounds-checks every index and span and proves
acyclicity with the one monotone check that children-before-parents emission
buys. Reusing it means a compound arriving from a handler is validated by the
same decoder that validates a value leaving for the host, instead of by a second
decoder written to the same specification and trusted to agree with the first.

A handler returns a string the same way it receives one, writing a pointer and a
length into the out-slot. The runtime copies those bytes into the session
scratch, so the value stays valid after the handler returns and the host keeps
no obligation once the call ends. A compound return uses that same scratch and
that same out-slot, with the bytes being a value image the runtime materialises
into its value arena. Exhausting the scratch is a named refusal, like every
other lent bound here, and so is a returned image the decoder rejects, which
keeps a handler that produced something unusable distinct from one that failed.

The failure modes stay distinguishable. A `Raw` under a null host is a named
refusal rather than a crash, and a non-zero result code from the host propagates
as an evaluation error, so a host that refuses an operation is never confused
with a host that produced a value.

## Intended, not shipped

- The compile-stage counterpart, where a handler services a macro operation at
  its binding time. That is the same discipline aimed at the other stage.
- The reuse analysis that produces the per-site uniqueness bit the update path
  reads. The runtime side is designed for it; the emit-time verdict is not built,
  and its derivation from the lease masks the check pass already computes is
  intended rather than demonstrated.
- The remaining Core forms (`Match`, `Iter`, `Interp`, `Handle`). `Match`'s arms
  carry no pattern representation yet, which is an undecided Core design rather
  than an unbuilt one. `Handle` is what non-local control flow (`return`,
  `break`, `continue`, and the loop forms) lowers to, and it needs only the
  non-resuming discharge for that.
- Record-shape resolution, owed by the compile side. The fast field-access
  strategies all need the record's shape named at the access site: a fixed offset
  needs it statically, an inline cache needs a shape id to guard on. Until the
  emitter produces one, the runtime's dynamic lookup is the whole story.
- The `Bytecode` tier over the CFG-of-blocks form.
- Diagnostics mapped to the ABI diagnostic shape.

## Non-scope

- The ABI contract itself (`vehje-runtime-abi`).
- The compiler-side dispatch and the embedding of this artifact
  (`vehje-runtime-driver`).
- Any build-environment semantics. The effect proof discharges them before the
  residual is emitted.

## Build integration

- `build.zig` drives the Zig compilation.
- Output: `zig-out/lib/libvehje_runtime.{so|dylib|dll}`.
- Target-triple-specific builds; a distribution bundle pairs the Rust compiler
  binary with the matching dylib for each supported platform.
- The `.zig` tree is not built by the Rust workspace tooling. It is validated by
  running `zig build` and `zig build test`.

## Dependencies

- The standard Zig library only. No third-party Zig packages, and no runtime
  allocator: memory is host-lent.

